window.__SLIDES&&window.__SLIDES('b1',{"s":["<section id=\"b1-tldr\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · 2016–2026</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Teammate inference, joined late by a population line</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px\">\n<div style=\"width:880px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Background · before the series</p><ul style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\"><li><b>Ad hoc teamwork</b> · Stone et al. 2010 posed cooperating with unknown teammates; HBA picks from guessed types.</li><li><b>Hand-built agent models</b> · Bayesian theory of mind and inverse RL (Baker) assumed noisy-rational agents.</li><li><b>Opponent modelling</b> · Foerster and Everett assumed known parametric opponents; Raileanu assumed they match oneself.</li><li><b>Partner populations</b> · FCP and MEP train one agent against a pool of self-play partners; HSP gives them hidden rewards.</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Problem</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Cooperate with an unseen teammate whose policy is hidden and may change, using only what its behaviour reveals.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Idea</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Compress the teammate&#x27;s behaviour into a type, code or belief and condition on it; a population line (MEP, HSP, GAMMA) supplies partners to train on.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<img src=\"assets/img/b1_tldr.webp\" alt=\"Lineage of the key papers in this work tree, drawn by year with arrows from parent to child\" style=\"width:736px;height:260px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">Solid arrows are inherited methods; white guests MEP, HSP, GAMMA feed the last two. White pills are guests: papers whose home is another tree, included because they sit on a lineage here.</p>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;background:#EFEBE0;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Terms</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#24487A\">Ad hoc teamwork</b> · Cooperate with unknown teammates, no joint training</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#24487A\">Teammate type</b> · A named behaviour pattern a teammate may follow</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#24487A\">Latent code</b> · Short vector summarising a teammate&#x27;s hidden behaviour</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\"><b style=\"color:#24487A\">Ego agent</b> · The agent being trained; its partner is unknown</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Inferring teammates</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">20 papers (3 guests) · 2016–2026 · ToMnet → LILI → Latent strategies</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-assume\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · Assumptions</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Teammate inference assumes a hidden, inferable partner</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:12px\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#24487A\">Hidden partner policy</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">The partner&#x27;s policy is never given; it is inferred from behaviour, as a guessed type or a learned code.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#24487A\">A training pool or prior</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">A pool or prior over partners: PLASTIC, AATEAM, ODITS, PACE keep pools; MEP, HSP, GAMMA build them; TAGET uses logs.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#24487A\">Partner behaviour is visible</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">ToMnet and MeLIBA see the partner fully; LIAM, ODITS, PACE and TAGET act on a local view only.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#24487A\">Mostly stable partners</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Fixed in LIAM, ODITS, HSP; Behavior switch, MeLIBA, Influence (offline), Latent strategies relax it.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start;padding-bottom:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"width:500px;font-size:28px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25;color:#24487A\">Small simulated teams</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:26px;line-height:1.3;color:#16212F\">Mostly simulated, scripted or RL partners; humans in LILI, MESH, Influence, MEP, HSP, GAMMA, Latent strategies, CooT.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:20px;align-items:center\"><div style=\"width:760px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Builds on</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3\">Bayesian games, theory of mind, meta-learning and partner-pool training; 12 of 46 links between members inherit a formalism, method or part.</p></div><svg width=\"48\" height=\"30\" viewBox=\"0 0 48 30\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,9.0 L28.799999999999997,9.0 L28.799999999999997,0 L48.0,15.0 L28.799999999999997,30.0 L28.799999999999997,21.0 L0,21.0 Z\" fill=\"#24487A\"/></svg><div style=\"width:816px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">So the series achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:26px;line-height:1.3\">From picking among types (PLASTIC) to latent codes (LIAM, ODITS), offline learning (TAGET) and in-context adaptation (CooT, on HSP&#x27;s pool).</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Inferring teammates</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">20 papers (3 guests) · 2016–2026 · ToMnet → LILI → Latent strategies</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-method\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · Evolution</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">From picking a type to inferring a latent partner code</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 32px\"><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.35\">12 of 46 links inherit: 6 inside the original line, 6 from guests MEP, HSP, GAMMA, which feed Latent strategies and CooT.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px\">\n<img src=\"assets/img/b1_method.webp\" alt=\"Lineage of every paper in the work tree by year, arrows from parent to child\" style=\"width:1664px;height:560px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">Each arrow points from a paper to the paper that builds on it. Dark pills mark the key papers. White pills are guests: papers whose home is another tree, included because they sit on a lineage here. Papers without an arrow are tied to the series only by baseline comparisons.</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Inferring teammates</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">20 papers (3 guests) · 2016–2026 · ToMnet → LILI → Latent strategies</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-stages\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · Stages</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">The series unfolds in three stages</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:16px;align-items:stretch\"><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #C8651B;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#C8651B\">1 · Choose among types</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2017–2023</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3EBF7;border:2px solid #24487A;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">PLASTIC</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3EBF7;border:2px solid #24487A;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">AATEAM</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3EBF7;border:2px solid #24487A;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Behavior switch</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3EBF7;border:2px solid #24487A;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">OEATE</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3EBF7;border:2px solid #24487A;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">CTCAT</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3EBF7;border:2px solid #24487A;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">MESH</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">PLASTIC reuses past policies; AATEAM adds attention; Behavior switch spots switches; OEATE fits parameters; CTCAT fixes bias; MESH matches strategies.</p></div><svg width=\"40\" height=\"28\" viewBox=\"0 0 40 28\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,8.4 L24.0,8.4 L24.0,0 L40.0,14.0 L24.0,28.0 L24.0,19.599999999999998 L0,19.599999999999998 Z\" fill=\"#24487A\"/></svg><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #3E6FD9;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#3E6FD9\">2 · Embed the partner</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2018–2024</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3EBF7;border:2px solid #24487A;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">ToMnet</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3EBF7;border:2px solid #24487A;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">MeLIBA</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3EBF7;border:2px solid #24487A;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">LIAM</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3EBF7;border:2px solid #24487A;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">ODITS</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3EBF7;border:2px solid #24487A;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">LILI</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3EBF7;border:2px solid #24487A;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Influence</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3EBF7;border:2px solid #24487A;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">GAMMA</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">ToMnet, MeLIBA: character code, belief; LIAM, ODITS: own-view and team codes; LILI, Influence: strategy z; GAMMA: sampled partner z.</p></div><svg width=\"40\" height=\"28\" viewBox=\"0 0 40 28\" style=\"flex:none;display:block;\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M0,8.4 L24.0,8.4 L24.0,0 L40.0,14.0 L24.0,28.0 L24.0,19.599999999999998 L0,19.599999999999998 Z\" fill=\"#24487A\"/></svg><div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-top:8px solid #8E4FC9;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 32px\"><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.15;color:#8E4FC9\">3 · Pools, probing, offline</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3;color:#5B6472\">2023–2026</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:10px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3EBF7;border:2px solid #24487A;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">MEP</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3EBF7;border:2px solid #24487A;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">HSP</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3EBF7;border:2px solid #24487A;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">PACE</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3EBF7;border:2px solid #24487A;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">TAGET</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3EBF7;border:2px solid #24487A;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">Latent strategies</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:500;background:#E3EBF7;border:2px solid #24487A;border-radius:999px;padding:6px 16px\">CooT</p></div><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.35\">MEP and HSP build partner pools; PACE rewards probing; TAGET and CooT use transformers; Latent strategies clusters a GAMMA-style VAE.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Inferring teammates</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">20 papers (3 guests) · 2016–2026 · ToMnet → LILI → Latent strategies</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s01\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · Belief &amp; truth 2016 · 1 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">When can you trust guesses about other agents?</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Outside works: Harsanyi&#x27;s Bayesian games, Kalai and Lehrer, HBA (Albrecht and Ramamoorthy). No earlier member; CTCAT later reuses its game model.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Users supply a finite set of guessed types</li><li>True types are in the set (dropped in Secs. 6-7)</li><li>One controlled agent; state and actions fully observed</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Three belief updates with conditions for being correct, ten automatic priors, when wrong types still finish the task, and a statistical type test.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s01.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:154px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">CTCAT reviews its stochastic Bayesian game as its problem formalism; Behavior switch and MeLIBA only cite it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Priors shift long-run payoff over 78 repeated 2x2 games; the type test is near 100% on random behaviours (paper&#x27;s wording).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Belief and Truth in Hypothesised Behaviours</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Albrecht et al. · AIJ 2016</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s02\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · PLASTIC 2017 · 2 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">PLASTIC adapts to new teammates by reusing past ones</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Outside works: ad hoc teamwork theory, UCT planning, Fitted Q Iteration, transfer learning. No earlier member; AATEAM extends it.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Known environment dynamics, visible state</li><li>Past experience with teammates; teammates stationary</li><li>New teammates resemble old ones (not enforced)</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Learns a model or policy per past teammate, tracks which fits via a loss-bounded belief update, then plans with UCT or follows that policy.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s02.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">AATEAM keeps its per-type policies and HFO setup but swaps similarity-based switching for attention; ODITS and CTCAT cite it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Pursuit, unseen student teams: 13.36 vs 9.97 prey caught per 500 steps when copying the teammates (Fig. 9).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Making friends on the fly: Cooperating with new teammates</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Barrett et al. · AIJ 2017</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s03\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · ToMnet 2018 · 3 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">A neural net learns to model other agents&#x27; minds</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Outside works only: Bayesian theory of mind, inverse RL, meta-learning, one-shot imitation. It replaces hand-built noisy-rational models with a learned one.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Observer sees the full state and all actions</li><li>11x11 gridworlds, one agent per episode</li><li>Many agents available to meta-train on</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Character net, mental-state net and prediction net, meta-learned end to end: weights are the prior, the embedding is the agent-specific posterior.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s03.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:246px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">MeLIBA adopts its character vs mental-state split; LIAM, OEATE, LILI and Latent strategies only cite it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Comparable to Bayesian inverse RL on goal-driven agents (Fig. 4c); passes a Sally-Anne false-belief test. The ICML paper gives no headline number.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Machine Theory of Mind</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Rabinowitz et al. · ICML 2018</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s04\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · Behavior switch 2019 · 4 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Teammates may switch behavior; a CNN spots the switch</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Outside works: type-based ad hoc teamwork (Albrecht and Stone 2017) plus Bayesian change point detection. Relaxes the one-fixed-type assumption. No member precedes it.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Known finite type set; the true type is in it</li><li>Teammate actions visible; switches at exogenous times</li><li>Type models plus a simulator to train the detector</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Draws type likelihoods over the last n_t steps as an image; a CNN labels no change or old-to-new type, then the belief is reset.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s04.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:154px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">ODITS uses its ConvCPD detector only as a baseline; CTCAT and PACE only cite it. No member extends the method.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">88% change-point accuracy at window 20 and fewer steps to finish than Bayesian CPD, in a modified predator-prey grid (Table 2, Fig. 3).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Ad Hoc Teamwork with Behavior Switching Agents</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Ravula et al. · IJCAI 2019</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s05\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · AATEAM 2020 · 5 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">AATEAM blends per-type attention networks each step</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Extends PLASTIC-Policy: keeps per-type learned policies and the HFO setup; replaces similarity-based switching with attention networks.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Plenty of data for each past teammate type</li><li>Teammate actions not seen; only states observed</li><li>Small set of high-level actions, simulator</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">One attention network per past type; each also predicts the next state, and a softmax over match gaps blends their action odds.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s05.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">ODITS compares against it as a type-based baseline; TAGET only cites it. No member extends it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">HFO 2v2: beats PLASTIC-Policy significantly on about 8 of 10 teammate types; the paper gives no numeric table.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">AATEAM: Achieving the Ad Hoc Teamwork by Employing the Attention Mechanism</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Chen et al. · AAAI 2020</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s06\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · LILI 2020 · 6 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Learn the other agent&#x27;s latent strategy, then steer it</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Outside works: hidden-parameter MDPs, SAC, LILAC. Keeps LILAC&#x27;s latent view of non-stationarity but lets the ego agent&#x27;s actions shape it. No member precedes it.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Partner follows a latent strategy, fixed within one interaction</li><li>Intent hidden; ego sees only its own states and rewards</li><li>Repeated one-on-one meetings; strategy inferable from history</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Encoder maps the last interaction to a guess of the partner&#x27;s next strategy z; a decoder rebuilds transitions and rewards; SAC acts on z.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s06.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:200px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Influence (offline) adopts its encoder/decoder idea; PACE reuses its multi-episode return and also compares against it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Real air hockey vs a robot striker: blocks 91% of the last 100 shots vs 44% for SAC (Sec. 5.3, Fig. 6).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Learning Latent Representations to Influence Multi-Agent Interaction</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Xie et al. · CoRL 2020</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s07\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · MeLIBA 2021 · 7 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">A learned belief over others lets agents adapt online</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on ToMnet: adopts its character vs mental-state split, made probabilistic. Also IBRL, VariBAD-style meta-learning; contrasts LIOM and RL2.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Known prior over others; full observability in game</li><li>Others adapt within a game; memory resets each game</li><li>No control over others; only own self-interested reward</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Hierarchical sequential VAE tracks a belief over each partner&#x27;s character and mental state; a PPO policy conditions on the belief mean and variance.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s07.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:292px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Latent strategies follows its future-action-predicting VAE decoder; ODITS, CTCAT, TAGET compare or cite only.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Treasure Hunt return 10.1 vs 7.7 for RL2 and 1.3 for a non-adaptive policy (Table 3a); ties LIOM (10.2) and MeLIBA-m (10.0).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Deep Interactive Bayesian Reinforcement Learning via Meta-Learning</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Zintgraf et al. · AAMAS 2021</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s08\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · LIAM 2021 · 8 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Model other agents using only your own local view</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Outside works: neural agent models (He, Grover) and A2C. Cites ToMnet, MeLIBA and LILI only as related work, which it contrasts.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>K fixed hidden policies, unchanged within an episode</li><li>Only own observations and actions at execution</li><li>Partner must be reactive; vector observations only</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Encoder reads the own history into z; in training a decoder rebuilds the other agent&#x27;s observation and action. Only the encoder runs at execution, feeding A2C.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s08.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:200px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">PACE and CTCAT compare against it; TAGET adapts it as LIAM-off; CooT only cites it. No member inherits its method.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Closer to the full-information bound FIAM than to the no-model NAM in all 3 games; &gt;90% action accuracy after 10 steps (Fig. 3, 4b).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Agent Modelling under Partial Observability for Deep Reinforcement Learning</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Papoudakis et al. · NeurIPS 2021</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s09\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · ODITS 2022 · 9 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">An ad hoc agent infers the teamwork situation online</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Type-based AHT: compares with PLASTIC, AATEAM, ConvCPD as baselines; cites MeLIBA as needing full views. Learns a code instead of fixed types.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Teammates sampled from a pool; 8 train, 7 unseen test</li><li>Full state only in training; local view at execution</li><li>One shared team reward; teams of 2 to 8 agents</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Encoder learns a teamwork-situation code from full state; a proxy encoder infers it from local data via a mutual-information loss; it sets utility-net weights.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s09.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:154px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">CTCAT compares against it; TAGET adapts it as ODITS-off; PACE only cites it. No member inherits its method.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Best in all 6 scenarios; ~0.80 vs ~0.51 captured preys over the best baseline in Predator Prey 4d2y (read from Fig. 3 curves).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Online Ad Hoc Teamwork under Partial Observability</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Gu et al. · ICLR 2022</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s10\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · OEATE 2022 · 10 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">OEATE infers teammates&#x27; parameters from finished tasks</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Outside works: Albrecht and Stone&#x27;s AGA/ABU (baselines), UCT-H, POMCP. Cites PLASTIC-Model and ToMnet only as related work.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Given set of types; parameters in a fixed range</li><li>Teammates do not learn; no communication channel</li><li>The agent sees which task each teammate finishes</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Keeps N estimators per teammate and type, each predicting its next task; scores them on finished tasks and breeds new ones from past winners.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s10.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Lower parameter error than AGA, ABU and POMCP on every grid size, 20x20 to 45x45 (p &lt; 0.001; foraging); type error better in most cases.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">On-line estimators for ad-hoc task execution: learning types and parameters of teammates for effective teamwork</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Yourdshahi et al. · JAAMAS 2022</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s11\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · MESH 2022 · 11 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">A robot matches its human partner&#x27;s team strategy</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Outside works: Carroll et al.&#x27;s human proxy, ad hoc teaming (Barrett), apprenticeship learning. No solid edge in this tree; Latent strategies cites it.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Strategies are discrete, team-level clusters</li><li>Human data holds a fair sample of strategies</li><li>Copying the partner&#x27;s strategy helps</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">HMM plus k-means find strategies in human-human teams; one PPO policy per strategy; online belief weights blend them by how well each predicts the partner.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s11.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:62px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Latent strategies cites it for strategy models plus online mixture of experts, but does not build on it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Overcooked, 125-person study: more orders served than the pooled-proxy baseline in 2 of 5 kitchens.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Coordination With Humans Via Strategy Matching</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Zhao et al. · IROS 2022</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s12\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · CTCAT 2023 · 12 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Type-based ad hoc agents can pick the worst response</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Reuses Albrecht et al.&#x27;s stochastic Bayesian game as formalism; compares against MeLIBA, LIAM, ODITS. Adds causal inference, not their mechanism.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Teammates form types; one best response per type</li><li>Instance is the only confounder and is observable</li><li>Cooperative, shared reward, no talking; 2 players</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Reweights each instance&#x27;s reward by p(policy|type) / p(policy|type, instance) so Q-learning is unbiased despite skewed teammate data.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s12.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:246px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">~96% optimal-policy choice vs ~34-55% for baselines in goal-based predator-prey (Fig. 3, read off the plot); fixes two real Simpson cases.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Controlling Type Confounding in Ad Hoc Teamwork with Instance-wise Teammate Feedback Rectification</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Xing et al. · ICML 2023</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s13\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · MEP 2023 · 13 of 20 · Guest</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Guest: a max-entropy partner pool for human play</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Home: Population training for humans tree. Here it is the pool builder that HSP, GAMMA and Latent strategies reuse. Outside: FCP, TrajeDi, PBT.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Two-player cooperative game; no human data in training</li><li>Test partners (humans) are unseen in training</li><li>Pool of 5 agents plus one best-response agent</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">A population-entropy bonus makes pool agents differ; the final agent trains against the pool, sampling hard-to-cooperate partners more often.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s13.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:200px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">HSP, GAMMA and Latent strategies reuse its policies or sampling; CooT compares against it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Overcooked, 5 layouts: top reward with a human proxy in all five (Fig. 4a); on par with human-human play with real people (Fig. 5). Bars only.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Maximum Entropy Population-Based Training for Zero-Shot Human-AI Coordination</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Zhao et al. · AAAI 2023</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s14\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · HSP 2023 · 14 of 20 · Guest</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Guest: hidden-reward partners for biased humans</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Home: Population training for humans tree. Reuses MEP policies as half its partner pool; CooT builds on its hidden-utility setting.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Humans follow fixed policies with hidden event rewards</li><li>Two-player Overcooked; pool of 36 partner policies</li><li>No human data in training</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Gives each training partner a random hidden reward over game events, filters duplicates, then trains one adaptive agent on that pool.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s14.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">CooT formulates its Hidden-Utility Markov Game from it and reuses its partner population; GAMMA reuses its PPO code.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Distant Tomato, tomato-first script: 277.9 vs at most 23.3 for FCP, MEP, TrajDiv (Tab. 2); Many Orders with humans: 382.5 vs at most 323.5 (Tab. 4).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Learning Zero-Shot Cooperation with Humans, Assuming Humans Are Biased</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Yu et al. · ICLR 2023</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s15\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · Influence (offline) 2023 · 15 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Offline RL learns to steer suboptimal human partners</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Adopts LILI&#x27;s latent-strategy encoder/decoder for offline human data and within-episode shifts; adds CQL stitching (COG-style).</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Only logged human-human data; no simulator or online trials</li><li>Humans suboptimal and passive; no influence in the data</li><li>Hidden strategy z may shift within an episode</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Relabels logged play with a new reward and trains CQL to stitch behaviours; an encoder over recent history gives z that conditions Q and policy.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s15.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:62px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Latent strategies cites it as prior work that encodes the partner&#x27;s trajectory; no member builds on it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">With real humans on a new task (Human Deliver): reward ~220 vs ~143 for filtered BC (Fig. 5, read from bars, about +-5).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Learning to Influence Human Behavior with Offline Reinforcement Learning</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Hong et al. · NeurIPS 2023</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s16\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · PACE 2024 · 16 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Agents learn to probe an unknown peer, then adapt</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Reuses LILI&#x27;s multi-episode return objective; LILI, LIAM and its LIAMX variant are also baselines. Other members only cited.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Diverse peer pool in training; peer index known only then</li><li>Same peers over several episodes (5 to 100 here)</li><li>Scripted peers in simulators; no human peers</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Pays a peer-identification bonus (the identifier&#x27;s probability on the true peer) so the agent explores when unsure; context encoder plus PPO.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s16.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:246px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">CooT compares against it (adapted onto HSP); Latent strategies lists it as an extra baseline; TAGET only cites it.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">PO-Overcooked success 0.553 vs 0.054-0.111 for baselines (Table 3); falls to 0.173 without the exploration reward (Table 2).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Fast Peer Adaptation with Context-aware Exploration</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Ma et al. · ICML 2024</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s17\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · GAMMA 2024 · 17 of 20 · Guest</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Guest: a VAE over partner populations samples partners</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Home: Population training for humans tree. Reuses MEP populations as VAE data and HSP&#x27;s PPO code (both). Outside: FCP, CoMeDi, PPO-BC.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Diverse joint-trajectory data from simulated or human play</li><li>Two players; partner style lives in a latent z</li><li>Humans cover a wide range of styles</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">A VAE over joint trajectories; a fresh latent z each episode is a new partner; a PPO Cooperator trains on them, with human-adaptive sampling.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s17.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:108px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Latent strategies builds on its VAE-over-population idea and compares against it; CooT runs it as an extra baseline.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Overcooked, 80 humans: GAMMA-HA 91.1 / 93.1 vs best baseline 76.2 / 85.3 (Counter Circuit / Multi-strategy; Table 3).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Learning to Cooperate with Humans using Generative Agents</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Liang et al. · NeurIPS 2024</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s18\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · TAGET 2025 · 18 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Ad hoc teamwork learned from a fixed offline dataset</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Compares against ODITS-off and LIAM-off; cites AATEAM, MeLIBA, PACE as background. Its backbone, the Decision Transformer, is outside the tree.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Offline logged data only; no simulator or exploration</li><li>Data from 3 MARL populations; test teammates from a 4th</li><li>Local view at deployment; two-agent cooperative grids</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Two-level Decision Transformer: a team encoder predicts team return and a future global state as sub-goal; a proxy encoder works from the ego view.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s18.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:246px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 24px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Next in the series</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">CooT cites it as offline AHT needing sub-goal supervision and lacking online adaptation.</p></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Best return in all 6 test settings; +37.83% average over the best baseline (Table 1); the PP-8 entry looks inconsistent (see notes).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Ad Hoc Teamwork via Offline Goal-Based Decision Transformers</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Zhang et al. · ICML 2025</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s19\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · Latent strategies 2025 · 19 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">A cooperator that infers partner strategy mid-episode</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on GAMMA&#x27;s VAE over population trajectories; reuses MEP&#x27;s priority sampling and MeLIBA&#x27;s future-action decoder.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Hidden partner strategy that may switch mid-episode</li><li>Offline agent-agent data; no human data in training</li><li>Partners resemble training strategies; simulator available</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">A VAE over population trajectories gives an 8-d latent; K-means clusters become strategy types; Fixed Share re-weights them each step.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s19.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:292px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Open layout: 842 vs 574 for GAMMA (BP population, Table 1); best in 3 of 4 layouts; higher team score with 119 humans (p = .003).</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Adaptively Coordinating with Novel Partners via Learned Latent Strategies</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Li et al. · NeurIPS 2025</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-s20\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · CooT 2026 · 20 of 20</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">CooT adapts to new partners from context alone</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:48px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:900px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:30px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Starts from</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">Builds on HSP&#x27;s hidden-utility setting and partner population; compares against MEP, GAMMA and PACE. Also Decision-Pretrained Transformer.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Assumes</p><ul style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\"><li>Partners with hidden event rewards</li><li>Best-response data from 36 HSP policies</li><li>Same partner for several episodes; no messages</li></ul></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Adds</p><p style=\"font-size:28px;line-height:1.3\">A GPT-2 transformer reads the last 5 episodes with a partner and outputs the best-response action; adaptation is context growth, no weight updates.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Place in the tree</p><img src=\"assets/img/b1_s20.webp\" alt=\"Papers this one builds on at left, this paper in the middle, papers that build on it at right\" style=\"width:716px;height:292px;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;background:#FFFFFF\"></div>\n\n</div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:16px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Achieves</p><p style=\"font-size:30px;line-height:1.3\">Overcooked, 5 layouts: BR-prox 0.57 vs 0.44 for HSP (Table 1); 36-person human study.</p></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">CooT: Learning to Coordinate In-Context with Coordination Transformers</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">Wang et al. · ICML 2026</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-eval\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · Evaluation</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">Tests grew from gridworlds to real human partners</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"width:560px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:20px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:10px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-radius:16px;padding:22px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472\">Benchmarks and baselines</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Early papers use small domains: Pursuit and HFO, ToMnet gridworlds, Treasure Hunt, predator-prey.</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Later: partial views, offline logs (Influence, TAGET), humans (MESH, MEP, HSP, GAMMA, Latent strategies, CooT).</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.3\">Each paper picks its own benchmark, baselines and metric; &quot;~&quot; marks values read off plots.</p></div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:6px;background:#E3EBF7;border-radius:16px;padding:18px 28px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Reading</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.35\">Benchmarks and metrics differ: do not rank rows. Read each as one paper&#x27;s win over its own baselines.</p></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-radius:16px;padding:20px 32px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5B6472;padding-bottom:4px\">Headline result of each paper</p><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#24487A\">PLASTIC</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Pursuit, unseen student teams</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">13.36 vs 9.97 prey per 500 steps when copying</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#24487A\">LILI</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Air hockey, robot striker</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">Blocks 91% of shots vs 44% for SAC</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#24487A\">GAMMA</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Overcooked, 80 humans, Counter Circuit</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">91.1 vs 76.2 for MEP (Table 3); 93.1 vs 85.3 on Multi-strategy</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#24487A\">CTCAT</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Goal-based predator-prey</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">~96% optimal choice vs ~34-55% baselines</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#24487A\">PACE</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">PO-Overcooked</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">0.553 success vs 0.054-0.111 for baselines</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#24487A\">TAGET</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">PP, LBF, Overcooked (offline)</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">+37.83% average return over best baseline</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#24487A\">Latent strategies</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Overcooked, Open layout</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">842 vs 574 for GAMMA</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:0px;padding:5px 0px;border-bottom:1px solid #DDD8CB\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;line-height:1.25\"><b style=\"color:#24487A\">CooT</b> · <span style=\"color:#5B6472\">Overcooked, 5 layouts</span></p><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.25\">BR-prox 0.57 vs 0.44 for HSP</p></div></div>\n</div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Inferring teammates</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">20 papers (3 guests) · 2016–2026 · ToMnet → LILI → Latent strategies</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>","<section id=\"b1-open\" style=\"background:#FAF8F3;color:#16212F;font-family:'IBM Plex Sans', Arial, sans-serif;padding:96px 128px 160px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:28px\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:4px\">\n<p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#24487A\">Inferring teammates · Open questions</p>\n<h2 style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:56px;font-weight:600;line-height:1.1;color:#16212F\">What the series still leaves open</h2>\n</div>\n<div style=\"display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:24px\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #24487A;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#24487A;width:56px\">1</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Partners are mostly scripted or pre-trained; real humans only in 8 members (LILI is a one-person pilot).</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #24487A;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#24487A;width:56px\">2</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Partners unlike the training pool are hard (Latent strategies, PACE, TAGET); MEP, HSP, GAMMA rely on pool coverage.</p></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:32px;align-items:center;background:#FFFEFB;border:1px solid #DDD8CB;border-left:8px solid #24487A;border-radius:16px;padding:28px 36px\"><p style=\"font-family:'Source Serif 4', Georgia, serif;font-size:64px;font-weight:600;line-height:1;color:#24487A;width:56px\">3</p><p style=\"flex:1;font-size:32px;line-height:1.35\">Teams are mostly two agents; CTCAT, TAGET and Latent strategies are 2-player only, ODITS reaches 8.</p></div></div>\n<div style=\"position:absolute;left:128px;bottom:56px;width:1664px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:2px\"><p style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:600;color:#16212F\">Inferring teammates</p><p style=\"font-size:24px;color:#5B6472\">20 papers (3 guests) · 2016–2026 · ToMnet → LILI → Latent strategies</p></div>\n\n</section>\n\n</section>"],"n":["Members (20, chronological): Belief &amp; truth 2016, PLASTIC 2017, ToMnet 2018, Behavior switch 2019, AATEAM 2020, LILI 2020, MeLIBA 2021, LIAM 2021, ODITS 2022, OEATE 2022, MESH 2022, CTCAT 2023, MEP 2023 (guest), HSP 2023 (guest), Influence (offline) 2023, PACE 2024, GAMMA 2024 (guest), TAGET 2025, Latent strategies 2025, CooT 2026. Guests: MEP, HSP, GAMMA, all from the tree Population training for humans. Each sits on a method-lineage edge to a member: MEP -&gt; HSP/GAMMA/Latent strategies, HSP -&gt; CooT/GAMMA, GAMMA -&gt; Latent strategies (kind 0 or 1); CooT also compares against MEP and GAMMA (kind 2). Edge counts: 46 edges, 5 kind 0, 7 kind 1, 34 kind 2 (before guests: 38 edges, 3/3/32). Inheritance (solid): kind 0: AATEAM -&gt; PLASTIC (per-type policies kept, attention replaces switching); MeLIBA -&gt; ToMnet (character vs mental-state split, made probabilistic); Influence (offline) -&gt; LILI (latent-strategy encoder/decoder, offline human data); NEW CooT -&gt; HSP (Hidden-Utility Markov Game &#x27;inspired by HSP&#x27;, trained on the HSP partner population); NEW Latent strategies -&gt; GAMMA (VAE over population trajectories, then clustered). Kind 1: Latent strategies -&gt; MeLIBA (future-action VAE decoder); PACE -&gt; LILI (multi-episode return objective); CTCAT -&gt; Belief &amp; truth (stochastic Bayesian game); NEW HSP -&gt; MEP (half the pool is MEP policies); NEW GAMMA -&gt; MEP (populations as VAE data); NEW GAMMA -&gt; HSP (PPO code); NEW Latent strategies -&gt; MEP (priority sampling). Comparison (kind 2) is the rest, including NEW CooT vs MEP and vs GAMMA (baselines). I checked each guest edge against the child&#x27;s text (CooT, GAMMA, HSP, Latent strategies). Story change: before guests the line was parallel attempts with few inherited links; now Latent strategies and CooT, the two newest members, reach back through GAMMA, HSP and MEP into the population-training line. The guests do no teammate inference at test time; they supply training partners (MEP, HSP) or a partner generator (GAMMA). Solid components: {PLASTIC, AATEAM}, {ToMnet, MeLIBA, Latent strategies, GAMMA, MEP, HSP, CooT}, {LILI, Influence, PACE}, {Belief &amp; truth, CTCAT}. Behavior switch, LIAM, ODITS, OEATE, MESH and TAGET have no solid edge. Figure: 8 nodes (ToMnet, LILI, MeLIBA, MEP, HSP, GAMMA, Latent strategies, CooT); PACE, AATEAM, LIAM are dropped. Shared problem: all members infer or prepare for a hidden partner. Survey vocabulary: Mirsky et al. 2022 ST2 and ST4; the guests are population-based training for zero-shot coordination.","Shared assumptions, with the paper that relaxes each: (1) Hidden partner policy, inferred from behaviour: all members. (2) A training pool or prior (guests: MEP builds a 5-agent entropy pool, HSP a 36-policy hidden-reward pool with half MEP, GAMMA a VAE over pool trajectories): ToMnet (many agents to meta-train on), PLASTIC and AATEAM (past teammate types), LIAM (K fixed policies), ODITS (8 train, 7 test teammates), PACE (diverse peer pool), TAGET and Influence (offline logs), Latent strategies (offline agent-agent data), CooT (36 HSP policies). Belief &amp; truth and Behavior switch need a given type set; OEATE needs a given type set with parameter ranges. (3) Visibility: ToMnet, MeLIBA, PLASTIC, AATEAM (states only) see the partner or the state fully; LIAM relaxes this to the own local view; ODITS, PACE, TAGET follow. (4) Stability (HSP assumes fixed partner policies): fixed within an episode in LIAM and ODITS (and OEATE); Behavior switch allows switches at exogenous times; MeLIBA assumes others adapt within a game; LILI lets strategy change between episodes; Influence (offline) and Latent strategies allow switches within an episode; PACE and CooT adapt across episodes. (5) Small simulated teams: mostly 2-agent grids; humans appear in MEP (AMT, count not found), HSP (60 volunteers), GAMMA (80 users), LILI (one-human pilot), MESH (125 participants), Influence (10 users), Latent strategies (119 participants), CooT (36 participants). Builds on: Belief &amp; truth cites Harsanyi, Kalai and Lehrer, HBA; ToMnet builds on Bayesian theory of mind, inverse RL, meta-learning; LILI on HiP-MDPs, SAC, LILAC; Influence on CQL; TAGET and CooT on Decision (Pretrained) Transformers. 12 of 46 links inherit anything (see &#x27;tldr&#x27; notes). Achieves: see per-paper steps; numbers are not comparable across papers. Counts: of 46 edges, 5 kind 0, 7 kind 1, 34 kind 2.","Stages (a reading aid, not a strict order): (1) Choose among types: Belief &amp; truth (theory of when guessed types can be trusted), PLASTIC (policy or model per past teammate, belief update, UCT or follow), AATEAM (attention net per type, blend by next-state match; kind 0 from PLASTIC), Behavior switch (CNN change-point detector), OEATE (estimators scored on finished tasks), CTCAT (causal reweighting; kind 1 from Belief &amp; truth&#x27;s game model). (2) Embed the partner: ToMnet (character and mental-state nets, meta-learned), MeLIBA (sequential VAE belief, PPO on belief; kind 0 from ToMnet), LIAM (encoder over own history, decoder rebuilds the other agent in training only), ODITS (teamwork-situation code, mutual-information proxy), LILI (encoder predicts next strategy z from last interaction). (3) Pools, probing, offline (this note lists the original grouping; Influence and GAMMA are shown in the Embed stage on the slide): Influence (offline) (kind 0 from LILI: CQL stitching plus latent-strategy encoder), MESH (HMM and k-means strategies, one policy each, belief mix), PACE (peer-identification reward; kind 1 from LILI&#x27;s multi-episode return), TAGET (Decision Transformer with sub-goal), Latent strategies (kind 1 from MeLIBA&#x27;s decoder; VAE clusters plus Fixed Share), CooT (transformer reads recent episodes, outputs best response). Method links relied on: AATEAM -&gt; PLASTIC, MeLIBA -&gt; ToMnet, Influence -&gt; LILI (kind 0); Latent strategies -&gt; MeLIBA, PACE -&gt; LILI, CTCAT -&gt; Belief &amp; truth (kind 1). All other pairs in the stages are parallel or compared as baselines. No ranking is implied by the stage order. The era labels are approximate: ToMnet (2018) and LILI (2020) are earlier than some members of the &#x27;type&#x27; stage (OEATE 2022, CTCAT 2023). Guest additions: MEP (population-entropy bonus, priority sampling), HSP (random hidden rewards, filtered pool, half MEP: kind 1 from MEP), GAMMA (VAE over pool trajectories, PPO Cooperator: kind 1 from MEP and HSP). Into this tree&#x27;s later members: Latent strategies (kind 0 from GAMMA, kind 1 from MEP) and CooT (kind 0 from HSP). Compared only (kind 2): CooT vs MEP and GAMMA.","Stages (a reading aid, not a strict order): (1) Choose among types: Belief &amp; truth (theory of when guessed types can be trusted), PLASTIC (policy or model per past teammate, belief update, UCT or follow), AATEAM (attention net per type, blend by next-state match; kind 0 from PLASTIC), Behavior switch (CNN change-point detector), OEATE (estimators scored on finished tasks), CTCAT (causal reweighting; kind 1 from Belief &amp; truth&#x27;s game model). (2) Embed the partner: ToMnet (character and mental-state nets, meta-learned), MeLIBA (sequential VAE belief, PPO on belief; kind 0 from ToMnet), LIAM (encoder over own history, decoder rebuilds the other agent in training only), ODITS (teamwork-situation code, mutual-information proxy), LILI (encoder predicts next strategy z from last interaction). (3) Pools, probing, offline (this note lists the original grouping; Influence and GAMMA are shown in the Embed stage on the slide): Influence (offline) (kind 0 from LILI: CQL stitching plus latent-strategy encoder), MESH (HMM and k-means strategies, one policy each, belief mix), PACE (peer-identification reward; kind 1 from LILI&#x27;s multi-episode return), TAGET (Decision Transformer with sub-goal), Latent strategies (kind 1 from MeLIBA&#x27;s decoder; VAE clusters plus Fixed Share), CooT (transformer reads recent episodes, outputs best response). Method links relied on: AATEAM -&gt; PLASTIC, MeLIBA -&gt; ToMnet, Influence -&gt; LILI (kind 0); Latent strategies -&gt; MeLIBA, PACE -&gt; LILI, CTCAT -&gt; Belief &amp; truth (kind 1). All other pairs in the stages are parallel or compared as baselines. No ranking is implied by the stage order. The era labels are approximate: ToMnet (2018) and LILI (2020) are earlier than some members of the &#x27;type&#x27; stage (OEATE 2022, CTCAT 2023). Guest additions: MEP (population-entropy bonus, priority sampling), HSP (random hidden rewards, filtered pool, half MEP: kind 1 from MEP), GAMMA (VAE over pool trajectories, PPO Cooperator: kind 1 from MEP and HSP). Into this tree&#x27;s later members: Latent strategies (kind 0 from GAMMA, kind 1 from MEP) and CooT (kind 0 from HSP). Compared only (kind 2): CooT vs MEP and GAMMA.","Albrecht, Crandall, Ramamoorthy, AIJ 2016. Starting point: Bayesian games (Harsanyi 1967), Kalai and Lehrer 1993, HBA, Barrett et al. 2011. Four parts: (1) posteriors, Sec. 4: product, sum and correlated posterior, Theorems 1-3 on when each is correct; (2) ten automatic priors, Sec. 5; (3) wrong types, Sec. 6: probabilistic bisimulation between the ideal process (true types) and the user process (guessed types); (4) a hypothesis test of a type, Sec. 7, alpha = 0.01. Numbers: Example 3 (Sec. 4.2) is a worked illustration, not an experiment: product posterior converges to 1.00, sum posterior to 2/3. Prior study: Rapoport and Guyer&#x27;s 78 strictly ordinal 2x2 games, 30 plays per game, 10 priors; priors significantly affect long-run payoff (paired t-tests at 5%, Fig. 2). Type test: 1,000 random-behaviour processes of 10,000 steps; accuracy often borders 100% (Fig. 6, bar heights only, no table). On adaptive type classes accuracy was mixed on wrong hypotheses. Edges used: CTCAT -&gt; Belief &amp; truth, kind 1 (CTCAT&#x27;s Sec. 3 reviews the stochastic Bayesian game of Harsanyi 1967 and Albrecht et al. 2016 as its framework; the method is new). Behavior switch and MeLIBA cite it (kind 2: Behavior switch lists it among type-based works; MeLIBA only in its reference list). Caveats: only repeated 2x2 matrix games and random or simple adaptive behaviours were tested; planning cost is exponential in players, actions and states.","Barrett, Rosenfeld, Kraus, Stone; AIJ 2017 (merges four earlier conference papers). Two variants: PLASTIC-Model (decision-tree model per past teammate, UCT planning) and PLASTIC-Policy (Fitted Q Iteration policy per past team, follows the most likely one). Numbers (Sections 5 and 6): pursuit 20x20, prey caught per 500 steps. Known hand-coded teammates GP 15.07 vs Match 6.12; PD 14.47 vs 2.60. Unseen student teams (StudentBroad, Fig. 9): 13.36 vs Match 9.97 (Match = copy the replaced teammate). HFO values (about 0.42 vs Random about 0.355) are read off Fig. 15 and approximate; the Combined policy 0.382 is stated. Edges used: AATEAM -&gt; PLASTIC, kind 0 (keeps per-type learned policies and HFO setup, replaces slow similarity-based switching with attention). OEATE -&gt; PLASTIC is kind 2 (related work: PLASTIC-Model learns types with C4.5 decision trees but ignores parameters). ODITS uses PLASTIC as a baseline, but that edge is not in this graph; CTCAT cites it as a type-based method. Caveats: the paper says PLASTIC may do arbitrarily badly if new teammates are far from past ones; whether HFO teams count as unseen is unclear because policies were learned for all 7 types.","Rabinowitz et al., ICML 2018. Edges used: MeLIBA -&gt; ToMnet, kind 0 (&quot;adopts the character vs mental-state latent split for agent models, extended probabilistically to interactive Bayes-optimal policy learning&quot;; the MeLIBA text says it builds on ideas from Rabinowitz et al. and calls its split a probabilistic extension). Kind 2 (related work or background only): LIAM (says ToMnet needs the modelled agent&#x27;s trajectories at execution), OEATE (ToMnet needs thousands of observations, tested on 11x11 grids), LILI (reference list), Latent strategies (listed among Theory-of-Mind models, &#x27;a separate but adjacent line&#x27;). Numbers: the ICML 10-page version has no results table and no headline accuracy. Fig. 4c: accuracy &#x27;comparable to&#x27; Bayesian IRL (16k sampled agents). Section 3.3: 120 deep-RL agents for training, 120 held out; Section 3.4: 40 and 40. Sally-Anne (Fig. 10): if the swap is in view the agent no longer returns to the old location; out of view its policy is unchanged, and the ToMnet predicts this. Plot values only read by eye. Assumptions: Section 2.1 and Discussion; the observer sees the MDP state and actions but not parameters, rewards, policies or identity; all experiments use full access (a stated limitation). Belief reports need supervision from the agents&#x27; reported beliefs. Uncertain: appendix (architecture, Figs. A1-A6) not read; extended arXiv version not read.","Behavior switch (Ravula, Alkoby, Stone; IJCAI 2019). Edges: ODITS -&gt; Behavior switch, kind 2 (ConvCPD as type-based baseline). CTCAT -&gt; Behavior switch, kind 2 (background: hand-built types, change point detection). PACE -&gt; Behavior switch, kind 2 (cited under adaptation to new agents). Behavior switch -&gt; Belief &amp; truth, kind 2 (cited among type-based works; the paper takes its notation from Albrecht and Stone 2017, outside the tree). Nothing in the tree inherits its method. Numbers (Table 2, exact): n_t=20: 88% accuracy, MSE 1.2; n_t=16: 72%, 2.4; n_t=14: 53%, 3.5; n_t=10: 22%, 4.2. Steps to finish (Fig. 3, read off bars, about +-1): perfect info ~25, ConvCPD-20 ~31, no information ~35, ConvCPD-10 ~39, Bayesian CPD ~40. 150 trials, 6 preys in the timing test. Short windows (n_t &lt;= 14) do worse than no information. Assumptions: Sections 3.1, 4.1, 5. Type = which prey the teammate chases; A* with prob 0.9, softmax temperature 2. CNN trained on 10,000 simulated likelihood matrices. Uncertain: number of change points per episode in the timing runs not given; number of preys for Table 2 not stated; only one CPD baseline and one simple domain.","Chen, Andrejczuk, Cao, Zhang; AAAI 2020. Edges used: AATEAM -&gt; PLASTIC, kind 0 (keeps per-type learned policies and HFO setup, replaces slow similarity-based switching with attention). Children: ODITS, kind 2 (AATEAM a type-based baseline); TAGET, kind 2 (cited as early online AHT assuming full observability). Also uses attention from Bahdanau et al. (outside the tree). How it works: for each past type j an RL policy (SARSA) is learned, 200,000 episodes of state-action pairs recorded, and one attention network (GRU encoder-decoder) trained. At play time each network also predicts the next state; weights are softmax(-10 x gap), the weighted action distributions are summed and the best action taken. 100 ms decision limit. Result: HFO 2 attackers vs 2 defenders, 5 past and 5 new teammate types, 10 trials of 1000 episodes. No results table. The count of 8 of 10 is derived from the statement that the difference is significant (95%, binomial test) except for axiom and ubc; the paper says &#x27;in most cases&#x27;. Plot values eyeballed in the deck notes (for example AATEAM about 0.75 vs PLASTIC about 0.71 on yushan) are approximate. Caveats stated by the paper: needs plenty of training data; unclear whether the architecture is the most appropriate; only 5+5 types.","LILI (Xie, Losey, Tolsma, Finn, Sadigh; CoRL 2020). Edges used: Influence (offline) -&gt; LILI, kind 0 (&quot;adopts LILI&#x27;s latent-strategy encoder/decoder idea, adapted to offline human data and within-episode strategy changes&quot;; Hong et al. also write that LILI is trained on scripted and heuristic partners and fixes z within an episode). PACE -&gt; LILI, kind 1 (PACE says its multi-episode return objective is similar to the one in Xie et al.; LILI is also a PACE baseline). LIAM -&gt; LILI, kind 2 (related work: LILI learns latents from local data but ignores the modelled agent&#x27;s trajectories). Latent strategies -&gt; LILI, kind 2 (cited with Hong et al. as the approach of encoding the partner&#x27;s trajectory, called brittle under train/test shift). LILI -&gt; ToMnet, kind 2 (reference list only). Numbers: robot striker, last 100 interactions: random 18%, SAC 44%, LILI 91%. Strike share with a left-block bonus: LILI 41/37/22% (left/middle/right); without influence 34/33/34% (read from Fig. 6 labels). Human expert, 40 shots: SAC 45% (18/40) vs LILI (no influence) 73% (29/40); the full LILI policy was too brittle against humans, so this pilot (one human) uses the no-influence variant. Simulation results are stated only qualitatively (LILI beats SAC, SLAC, LILAC in all four domains). Assumptions: Sections 1-3 and Appendix; dyads, fixed-length interactions, no centralised training or communication.","MeLIBA (Zintgraf et al., AAMAS 2021; arXiv v2 copy). Edges used: MeLIBA -&gt; ToMnet, kind 0 (see ToMnet). Children: Latent strategies, kind 1 (&quot;sequential future-action-predicting VAE decoder design follows MeLIBA&#x27;s insight&quot;, in its method section); kind 2: CTCAT (baseline), LIAM (related work: VAE agent model for fully observable tasks), ODITS (related work: belief needs full observations), PACE (related work, not compared), TAGET (related work, online only). MeLIBA -&gt; Belief &amp; truth is kind 2 (reference list only). Numbers (Table 3a, Treasure Hunt, &#x27;Meta-Test Performance&#x27;, 3 seeds, 95% CI): MeLIBA 10.1 (0.1), MeLIBA-m 10.0 (0.2), LIOM 10.2 (0.05), RL2 7.7 (0.3), average policy 1.3 (0.9). The paper itself says no significant difference between MeLIBA, MeLIBA-m and LIOM here. Game of Chicken: RL2 and MeLIBA reach Bayes-optimal play quickly; LIOM and MeLIBA-m lag (curves, no exact values). Runtime on Treasure Hunt: 5 h vs 12 h for RL2. Assumptions: Sections 2 and 6; the Discussion lists not needing the other agent&#x27;s observations at test time as future work. Only two small environments with hand-coded partners. Uncertain: the metric of Table 3a is read as average return; venue taken from the file name.","LIAM (Papoudakis, Christianos, Albrecht; NeurIPS 2021). Edges, all kind 2: LIAM -&gt; ToMnet, MeLIBA, LILI (related work; it says all of these either need the modelled agent&#x27;s trajectories at execution or are fully observable, except LILI, which does not use the modelled agent&#x27;s trajectories). Children: CTCAT (baseline), PACE (baseline plus its own LIAMX variant), TAGET (LIAM-off, baseline), CooT (cited as a few-shot method whose latent trajectory encoding may lose information; not a baseline). No kind 0/1 edge touches LIAM, so it joins the line by topic and by comparison. The paper has no explicit &#x27;we build on&#x27; sentence. Numbers: no results table. Fig. 3 ordering at end of training (read from curves): speaker-listener FIAM ~ LIAM &gt; CBAM &gt; NAM &gt; CARL &gt; VariBad; foraging FIAM &gt; LIAM &gt; NAM ~ CARL &gt; CBAM ~ VariBad; predator-prey FIAM &gt; LIAM &gt; NAM &gt; others. Sec. 4.4: &gt;90% accuracy on the other agent&#x27;s action and the own colour after 10 steps (double speaker-listener). Assumptions: Sec. 3.1, 3.2 and Conclusion. Others are not learning; behaviour outside the policy set is untested; image observations are a stated limitation. Uncertain: the tail ordering of CBAM, VariBad, CARL in predator-prey is hard to read.","ODITS (Gu, Zhao, Hao, An; ICLR 2022). Edges used, all kind 2: ODITS -&gt; Behavior switch (ConvCPD baseline), AATEAM (baseline), MeLIBA (related work: MeLIBA&#x27;s belief update needs full observations). PLASTIC is also a baseline in the paper, but no PLASTIC edge exists in this graph. Children: CTCAT (baseline), TAGET (ODITS-off baseline), PACE (cited for value decomposition with teammate context). Numbers: no results table; values read from the end of Fig. 3 curves (about +-0.02 Predator Prey, +-0.03 Save the City). Predator Prey 4d2y captured preys: ODITS ~0.80, AATEAM ~0.51, ConvCPD ~0.43, PLASTIC ~0.40, Random ~0.37, Combined ~0.31. Save the City 4a3b buildings: ODITS ~2.08 vs Combined ~1.82. QMIX comparison rough: ~0.77 and ~2.07 vs ~0.57 and ~1.78. Setup: 8 train and 7 test teammate policies chosen from 60 candidates per scenario; 4 seeds. Baselines were given partial observations (originals assume full visibility). Limits: small teams, mostly pre-trained RL teammates, effect of training-set diversity unexplored.","OEATE (Yourdshahi et al.; JAAMAS 2022). Edges, both kind 2 (related work): OEATE -&gt; PLASTIC (PLASTIC-Model learns types with C4.5 decision trees but ignores parameters), OEATE -&gt; ToMnet (needs thousands of observations; tested on 11x11 grids, OEATE scales to 45x45). Its main inspiration and baselines (Albrecht and Stone&#x27;s AGA and ABU, 2017) are outside this tree, and no member cites OEATE, so it has no solid edge: it joins the line by topic (type and parameter estimation). How it works (Sec. 6.2): each estimator holds parameter vector, success and failure scores and a predicted task; N = 100; an estimator is dropped when its success ratio is below 0.5; replacements are 20% random and the rest crossed over from bags of past successful parameter values. Planning with UCT-H (POMCP under partial views). Results (Sec. 7, mostly plots; no table of raw values): level-based foraging and capture the prey, 200 iterations, 20 runs per setting. Parameter error lower than AGA, ABU, POMCP in every test reported; type error better in most, worse (not significant) under partial view; tasks completed rarely differ. Table 5 says p &lt;= 0.041 for the grid-size figure while the text says p &lt; 0.001; the text is used. Assumptions: Sec. 2, 6.3-6.4, 8. Convergence theorem is proved for full observability only. Only &#x27;leader&#x27; types in the experiments.","MESH (Zhao, Simmons, Admoni; IROS 2022; venue taken from the file name, the PDF prints none). Edge: Latent strategies -&gt; MESH, kind 2 (related work: &#x27;Zhao et al. use apprenticeship learning to model the different types of observed strategies, then online mixture of experts to select compatible policies&#x27;; not compared). The pipeline has a similar shape to Latent strategies (cluster strategies, one policy per strategy, online weighting), which is my observation, not a claim of either paper. Its own roots (Carroll et al.) are outside this tree. Numbers (Sec. VII): 148 recruited on Prolific, 125 kept. Orders served significantly higher in Cramped Room (p = 0.009) and Forced Coordination (p &lt; 0.001); main effect F(1,124) = 23.285, p &lt; 0.001. Fluency gains broader: concurrent activity significant in 4 kitchens, robot idle time lower in all 5, subjective fluency in 3. Preference mixed: MESH in Forced Coordination (chi2 = 17.67), baseline in Coordination Ring (chi2 = 8.712). The paper states MESH does not underperform the baseline on task score in any kitchen. Method: reward weights from max-entropy IRL over 6 features; belief starts uniform, discounted memory of how well each strategy predicts the human. Caveats: only box plots, no raw mean scores in text; gamma value not given in the text read; the authors note matching strategies can sometimes do worse (simulated case).","CTCAT (Xing et al.; ICML 2023). Edges used: CTCAT -&gt; Belief &amp; truth, kind 1 (Sec. 3 reviews the stochastic Bayesian game of Harsanyi 1967 and Albrecht et al. 2016 as its framework; the rest of the method is causal inference and is new). Kind 2 (baselines): MeLIBA, LIAM, ODITS &#x27;compared as ad hoc teamwork baseline&#x27; (FIAM is also a baseline, outside the tree). Kind 2 (background): Behavior switch (hand-built types, change point detection). The paper argues all type-based methods, handcrafted (HBA, PLASTIC, AATEAM) or learned (LIAM, MeLIBA, ODITS), share the type confounding problem. Numbers: Fig. 3 (read off the plot, not a table): CTCAT ~95-97% proportion of optimal policy at observation lengths 5/10/15; baselines ~34-55%; text says the gap stays about 40%. Table 2 kidney stones, learned value open/closed: Q-learning 0.77/0.83, CTCAT 0.84/0.79. Table 3 magazine renewal Jan/Feb: Q-learning 0.51/0.65, CTCAT 0.54/0.50. The baseline picks the wrong option in both cases. Setup: 20x20 toroidal grid, 4 candidate policies, noise N(0, 0.1) on feedback in the real-data cases; DRQN in predator-prey, tabular Q-learning in the real cases. Uncertain: the lower rows of Tables 2/3 are read as learned value per option; Table 3 printed Feb total (9,157/5,869) looks like a typo. Only 2-player tasks.","Zhao et al.; AAAI 2023 (venue from file metadata; the PDF is the arXiv v3 preprint). Guest here; home tree: Population training for humans. It has no edge to an older member of this tree, so it enters as a source. Edges used: children HSP (kind 1: about half of the HSP policy pool is MEP policies trained on the game reward; HSP calls MEP the latest FCP variant), GAMMA (kind 1: MEP-generated populations are the VAE&#x27;s data, and GAMMA+MEP beats MEP alone), Latent strategies (kind 1: uses MEP&#x27;s priority-based partner sampling to train the cooperator; MEP populations supply the VAE data), CooT (kind 2: MEP is a population baseline, trained or loaded from ZSC-Eval). So MEP is inherited by three members but compared by one. Numbers: the paper gives rewards only as bar charts (Fig. 4a proxy, Fig. 4b ablation, Fig. 5 humans); no values are invented. Table 1 (Counter Circuit population entropy): alpha 0 -&gt; 0.988; 0.01 -&gt; 1.151; 0.04 -&gt; 1.791. Setup: Overcooked, 5 layouts, 400-step episodes, 5 seeds; baselines SP, PBT, TrajeDi, FCP (pool 10), MPD; Amazon Mechanical Turk humans, participant count not found. Caveats: later papers re-implement MEP with different settings (HSP uses a pool of 36 and uniform sampling, noting its MEP scores are higher than originally reported); numbers across papers are not comparable. MEP does no teammate inference at test time: its agent is a fixed best response to the pool. That is why it is a source of training partners for this tree, not a member of the inference line.","Yu et al.; ICLR 2023. Guest here; home tree: Population training for humans. It supplies the hidden-utility partner population that CooT trains on. Edges used: parents MEP (kind 1: half of the pool is MEP policies under the game reward; HSP&#x27;s ablation says to include a few MEP policies, esp. in Counter Circuit). Children: CooT (kind 0: &#x27;Hidden-Utility Markov Game inspired by HSP&#x27;; CooT collects best-response data from 36 HSP policies; HSP, HSP-ft and HSP-meta are its baselines), GAMMA (kind 1: the Cooperator&#x27;s PPO training code is built on the HSP implementation). Not an inference method: HSP learns one recurrent policy over the pool, with no explicit teammate code. Numbers (each paper&#x27;s own; mean over 5 seeds): Tab. 2 Distant Tomato, Tomato Placement script: FCP 15.6, MEP 20.1, TrajDiv 23.3, HSP 277.9. Tab. 4 Many Orders, humans: FCP 316.81, MEP 320.61, TrajDiv 323.52, HSP 382.52. 60 volunteers over 5 layouts; HSP preferred in the aggregate (Fig. 5: 0.53, 0.47, 0.53 vs MEP, FCP, TrajDiv). Caveats: the 277.9 result is against a script written by the authors; the human study is small; Appendix F.4.3 says humans rank MEP above HSP in Coordination Ring; in CooT&#x27;s own tests HSP is the strongest baseline (BR-prox 0.44) but CooT is on par in two layouts.","Hong, Levine, Dragan; NeurIPS 2023. Edge used: Influence (offline) -&gt; LILI, kind 0 (the paper says LILI is trained on scripted partners, assumes online data and a simulator, and fixes the strategy within an episode; it modifies LILI as &#x27;offline LILI&#x27; baseline too, and its latent-strategy objective is similar). Child: Latent strategies -&gt; Influence, kind 2 (cited with LILI for encoding the partner&#x27;s trajectory into a latent, and for human gameplay data as a possible source). It also builds on CQL and COG-style stitching, outside the tree. Numbers (all read from bar charts, about +-5; no numeric table): Fig. 5 with real humans, Human Deliver: BC ~90, filtered BC ~143, FCP ~135, offline RL ~220. Other columns offline RL/filtered BC: Asymm. Adv. ~170/~143, Forced Coord. ~124/~84, Tomato Bonus ~165/~84. Fig. 10: Open Asymm. Adv. offline RL ~155, offline LILI ~210, memory ~210, latent ~215; Counter Circuit latent ~195 vs ~175. Latent beats memory only slightly in Open Asymm. Adv. (error bars overlap). Setup: 10 human users, 30 trajectories of 400 steps per layout and method; no significance tests. Assumptions: Sections 3-6, Appendix A. Overcooked is much simpler than real collaboration; stitching and strategy tracking are studied separately.","PACE (Ma, Wang, Zhong, Zhu, Wang; ICML 2024). Edges used: PACE -&gt; LILI, kind 1 (the paper says its multi-episode return is &#x27;similar to the multi-episode return objective used in Xie et al.&#x27;; LILI is also a baseline that &#x27;implicitly encodes the opponent as environment dynamics&#x27;). Kind 2: LIAM (baseline; LIAMX is PACE&#x27;s own cross-episode variant), Behavior switch, MeLIBA, ODITS (related-work citations, not compared). Children, kind 2: Latent strategies (extra baseline, appendix C.1), CooT (baseline, adapted onto HSP), TAGET (cited for online methods struggling offline). Numbers (Table 3, mean over 3 seeds): PO-Overcooked success PACE 0.553 +-0.029, Generalist 0.111, GSCU 0.100, LIAMX 0.099, LILI 0.090, LIAM 0.054, Oracle 1.0. Kuhn Poker reward PACE 0.047 vs LIAMX 0.027; Predator-Prey-W -3.93 vs -4.34. Table 2 ablation: 0.553 vs 0.173 (no exploration reward) vs 0.143 (neither). Sudden peer change (Table 1): 0.435 vs 0.553 stationary. Setup: Kuhn Poker 100 episodes per peer, PO-Overcooked and Predator-Prey-W 5 episodes. Assumptions: Sections 3.1-3.4, 4.1, Limitation. No significance test beyond std over 3 seeds. Later papers report PACE doing poorly in their settings (Latent strategies: comparable to the population best response; CooT: Table 1 PACE 0.24), which concerns their settings, not this paper&#x27;s claim.","Liang, Chen, Gupta, Du, Jaques; NeurIPS 2024. Guest here; home tree: Population training for humans. It links the population line (MEP, HSP) to the latent-code line: it is a decoder-over-latent partner model like ToMnet or LIAM, but used to generate training partners, not to infer a live partner. Edges used: parents MEP (kind 1: MEP populations are the data source; MEP+GAMMA beats MEP alone, 88.3 vs 64.0 on Multi-strategy), HSP (kind 1: Cooperator PPO code built on the HSP implementation). Children: Latent strategies (kind 0: applies GAMMA&#x27;s VAE-over-trajectories idea, then clusters the latent space; GAMMA is the main baseline), CooT (kind 2: extra baseline, appendix F). Numbers (Table 3, human-AI team score, Counter Circuit / Multi-strategy): FCP 26.2 / 34.4, FCP+GAMMA 57.9 / 65.4, CoMeDi 56.9 / 27.1, CoMeDi+GAMMA 72.2 / 34.7, MEP 76.2 / 64.0, MEP+GAMMA 81.1 / 88.3, PPO+BC 53.5 / 85.3, PPO+BC+GAMMA 59.7 / 77.5, GAMMA-HA 91.1 / 93.1 (SE about 1 to 3). 80 Prolific users, 60-second games. Caveats: the text says +60% / +43% over the best baselines but Table 3 gives about +20% / +9%; the table values are used. GAMMA-HA vs PPO+BC on Multi-strategy is not significant (p = 0.43). GAMMA-HA mixes in a little human data. Which fine-tuning variant the main row uses is unclear.","TAGET (Zhang, Chan, Ye, Cai, Zhao; ICML 2025). Edges, all kind 2: ODITS (ODITS-off, baseline), LIAM (LIAM-off, baseline), AATEAM (early online AHT assuming full observability), MeLIBA (Bayesian inference for teammate modelling, online), PACE (cited for online methods struggling offline). Child: CooT -&gt; TAGET, kind 2. The paper says ODITS and LIAM are online AHT work; that they are &#x27;built on&#x27; is not stated, only that they are the baselines. Numbers (Table 1, average return +-95% CI over 50 episodes): Overcooked-4 TAGET 0.98 vs CQL 0.46, LIAM-off 0.42, Prompt-DT 0.40, ODITS-off 0.24, DT 0.16, MADT 0.12. Gain over best baseline: PP-4 +2.93%, PP-8 +8.01% (printed), LBF-4 +37.25%, LBF-8 +45.45%, Overcooked-4 +113.04%, Overcooked-8 +20.31%; these average to 37.83%. Uncertain: PP-8 is 62.9 vs 62.4, about +0.8%, not +8.01%, so the printed entry and the 37.83% average look inconsistent; LBF-4 CI overlaps the baselines; the meaning of the -4/-8 suffix (test teammate set size) is not sharply defined. Ablation: dropping the goal decoder hurts most (Fig. 5, curves only). Setup: two-agent Predator-Prey, Level-Based Foraging, Overcooked; baselines ODITS-off, LIAM-off, CQL, MADT are the authors&#x27; adaptations to offline AHT.","Latent strategies / TALENTS (Li et al.; NeurIPS 2025). Edges used: Latent strategies -&gt; MeLIBA, kind 1 (&quot;sequential future-action-predicting VAE decoder design follows MeLIBA&#x27;s insight&quot;: the text says the next-H-action decoder is &#x27;insight that follows from Zintgraf et al.&#x27;). Kind 2: PACE (extra baseline, appendix C.1), LILI and Influence (offline) (prior approach of encoding the partner&#x27;s trajectory into a latent and conditioning on it, said to be brittle under distribution shift), MESH (related work, &#x27;Zhao et al.&#x27;), ToMnet (Theory-of-Mind models, &#x27;separate but adjacent&#x27;). Guests now give its main inheritance: GAMMA -&gt; kind 0 (the paper says TALENTS &#x27;applies the same approach of GAMMA&#x27; but clusters the latent space; GAMMA is the main baseline, trained on the same trajectories); MEP -&gt; kind 1 (priority-based partner sampling from Zhao et al.; MEP populations supply the data). FCP, the tracking-regret Fixed Share algorithm and the E3T baseline stay outside the tree; Fixed Share is not GAMMA&#x27;s. Numbers (Table 1, Open layout, held-out set of 12 behavior-preference agents): FCP population TALENTS 710.4, GAMMA 616.7, BR 427.1; MEP 720.8, 682.4, 437.0; BP 842.4, 573.9, 469.9. Best in Open, Hallway and Ring for every population; Forced Coordination is a weak spot (BR higher); Ring BP is close to BR. Human study: 119 participants, team score F(2,166) = 5.76, p = .003. Appendix C.1, Table A.4 (preliminary) averages: TALENTS 605.88, E3T 548.66, GAMMA 374.10, BR 343.71, PACE 313.34. 11 clusters 865.82 vs 1 cluster 650.83 (different setup from Table 1). Assumptions: Sections 3, 4, 6.1. Generalisation limited by the VAE&#x27;s interpolation; agents slowed to human speed; all experiments two-player. Uncertain: Table A.4 does not state its training population; cluster counts per layout only given for Open (11); exact human scores not printed.","CooT (Wang, Chuang et al.; ICML 2026). Edges: CooT -&gt; HSP, kind 0 (guest; the Hidden-Utility Markov Game is &#x27;inspired by&#x27; HSP and the 36 best-response partners come from the HSP training population; HSP, HSP-ft and HSP-meta are baselines). Kind 2: MEP (guest; population baseline), GAMMA (guest; extra baseline, appendix F), PACE (a baseline, adapted onto HSP-meta with PACE&#x27;s peer identifier; CooT says PACE relies on matching unseen partners to training identities), LIAM (few-shot method whose latent trajectory encoding may lose information; not compared), TAGET (offline goal-based DT for AHT that needs sub-goal supervision and has no online adaptation). Its inheritance is HSP (guest, kind 0) plus the Decision-Pretrained Transformer (Lee et al., outside the tree). The paper also compares PLASTIC (Barrett 2015, appendix F), which is not linked to our PLASTIC member. Numbers, Table 1 (Overcooked overall BR-prox, 3 seeds): BC 0.40, BC-RNN 0.42, MEP 0.30, HSP 0.44, HSP-ft 0.44, HSP-meta 0.29, PACE 0.24, CooT 0.57; return CooT 68.79 vs HSP 56.16. CooT is on par, not better, in two layouts (Coordination Ring 38.30 vs 41.30; Asymmetric Advantages 129.48 vs 134.01). Football 3v1 goal rate 2.50 vs BC 1.97. Human study (Coordination Ring, 36 participants): return 63.5 vs MEP 53.0; best-partner votes 18 of 36. Partner switch: recovers in at most 6 episodes. Context ablation (Coordination Ring): 1/3/5/7 episodes give 33.87 / 34.36 / 41.11 / 36.04. Caveats: text lists a Blocked Coordination layout but Table 1 shows Counter Circuit (table names used); overlap between train and evaluation partner sets (App. B.7) not read in detail; HSP and MEP use handcrafted dense rewards, CooT only sparse rewards; only a demo page is linked.","Benchmarks, as in each paper&#x27;s notes. PLASTIC: Pursuit 13.36 vs 9.97 prey per 500 steps with unseen student teams when copying (Fig. 9). LILI: air hockey, blocks 91% vs 44% for SAC. MeLIBA: Treasure Hunt return 10.1 vs 7.7 (RL2) and 1.3 (average); ties LIOM 10.2. ODITS: ~0.80 vs ~0.51 preys (Predator Prey 4d2y, read from curves). CTCAT: ~96% vs ~34-55% (read from the plot). Influence (offline): ~220 vs ~143 reward with real humans (bar charts, +-5). PACE: PO-Overcooked 0.553 vs 0.054-0.111. TAGET: +37.83% average over the best baseline (the PP-8 entry inconsistent, see uncertain). Latent strategies: Open layout 842 vs 574 for GAMMA; 119-person study p = .003. CooT: BR-prox 0.57 vs 0.44 for HSP, 36-person study. Also: AATEAM beats PLASTIC-Policy on about 8 of 10 types (no table); Behavior switch 88% change-point accuracy; OEATE lower parameter error than AGA, ABU (p &lt; 0.001, all grid sizes); MESH more orders served in 2 of 5 kitchens (125 participants); LIAM &gt;90% action accuracy after 10 steps. These numbers come from different benchmarks, metrics and baselines and must not be ranked. Values marked ~ are read from plots. Open questions come from the notes and the Mirsky et al. and Albrecht and Stone surveys: partners outside the training pool, real humans, larger or open teams, partial observability, communication. Guests: MEP: bar charts only (no numbers in the paper); HSP: Distant Tomato 277.9 vs at most 23.3, Many Orders humans 382.5 vs at most 323.5; GAMMA: 91.1 / 93.1 vs 76.2 / 85.3 with 80 humans (text claims +60% / +43%, Table 3 gives about +20% / +9%). Not comparable with the others, and the three guests use different human studies. ODITS was dropped from the slide milestones to make room for GAMMA; its figures remain in its own step.","Benchmarks, as in each paper&#x27;s notes. PLASTIC: Pursuit 13.36 vs 9.97 prey per 500 steps with unseen student teams when copying (Fig. 9). LILI: air hockey, blocks 91% vs 44% for SAC. MeLIBA: Treasure Hunt return 10.1 vs 7.7 (RL2) and 1.3 (average); ties LIOM 10.2. ODITS: ~0.80 vs ~0.51 preys (Predator Prey 4d2y, read from curves). CTCAT: ~96% vs ~34-55% (read from the plot). Influence (offline): ~220 vs ~143 reward with real humans (bar charts, +-5). PACE: PO-Overcooked 0.553 vs 0.054-0.111. TAGET: +37.83% average over the best baseline (the PP-8 entry inconsistent, see uncertain). Latent strategies: Open layout 842 vs 574 for GAMMA; 119-person study p = .003. CooT: BR-prox 0.57 vs 0.44 for HSP, 36-person study. Also: AATEAM beats PLASTIC-Policy on about 8 of 10 types (no table); Behavior switch 88% change-point accuracy; OEATE lower parameter error than AGA, ABU (p &lt; 0.001, all grid sizes); MESH more orders served in 2 of 5 kitchens (125 participants); LIAM &gt;90% action accuracy after 10 steps. These numbers come from different benchmarks, metrics and baselines and must not be ranked. Values marked ~ are read from plots. Open questions come from the notes and the Mirsky et al. and Albrecht and Stone surveys: partners outside the training pool, real humans, larger or open teams, partial observability, communication. Guests: MEP: bar charts only (no numbers in the paper); HSP: Distant Tomato 277.9 vs at most 23.3, Many Orders humans 382.5 vs at most 323.5; GAMMA: 91.1 / 93.1 vs 76.2 / 85.3 with 80 humans (text claims +60% / +43%, Table 3 gives about +20% / +9%). Not comparable with the others, and the three guests use different human studies. ODITS was dropped from the slide milestones to make room for GAMMA; its figures remain in its own step."]});
